---
import "../styles/global.css";
import data from "../data/viz.json";

const { meta } = data;
const int = (x: number | null) => (x == null ? "–" : Math.round(x).toLocaleString("en-US"));
const pct = (x: number | null, d = 2) => (x == null ? "–" : `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)} %`);
const num = (x: number | null, d = 1) => (x == null ? "–" : x.toFixed(d));

const parties = ["CDU/CSU", "SPD", "GRÜNE", "FDP", "DIE LINKE", "AfD", "BSW"];
const topMonths = [...data.monthly]
  .filter((d) => d.sentences != null && d.sentences >= 2000 && d.share_a != null)
  .sort((a, b) => b.share_a - a.share_a)
  .slice(0, 15);
const unitsMonth = data.stance_units.filter((d) => d.grain === "month");
const unitsQuarter = data.stance_units.filter((d) => d.grain === "quarter");
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Bundestag Climate Explorer</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header class="top">
        <div>
          <h1>Heat Without Debate?</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Data understanding for the hypotheses: climate attention in the German Bundestag next to the physical climate
            signal and public attention, {meta.first_month} to {meta.last_month}. Descriptive only; H1 and H2 are tested in #12.
          </p>
        </div>
        <button class="theme" id="theme-toggle" type="button">Toggle theme</button>
      </header>

      {!meta.has_relevance && (
        <p class="note"><strong>No attention yet:</strong> run <code>src/02_relevance.py</code>, then
          <code>src/07_data_understanding.py</code> and <code>viz/export_data.py</code> again.</p>
      )}

      <div class="tiles">
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{int(meta.speeches)}</div><div class="label">speeches</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{(meta.sentences / 1e6).toFixed(2)} M</div><div class="label">speech sentences</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{int(meta.relevant_sentences)}</div><div class="label">climate-relevant sentences ({pct(meta.relevant_sentences / meta.sentences)})</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{int(meta.main_topic_speeches)}</div><div class="label">main-topic speeches</div></div>
        <div class="tile"><div class="value">{meta.months}</div><div class="label">months ({meta.months_with_sittings} with sittings)</div></div>
      </div>

      <nav class="toc">
        <a href="#h1">H1 signals</a><a href="#indexed">H1 indexed</a><a href="#trends">H1 Google Trends</a>
        <a href="#h2">H2 Germany vs world</a><a href="#h3">H3 parties</a><a href="#units">Stance units</a>
        <a href="#corpus">Corpus and join</a><a href="#top">Top months</a><a href="#notes">Notes</a>
      </nav>

      <section id="h1">
        <h2>H1: attention, public attention and the physical signal</h2>
        <p class="sub">Monthly values as faint bars, 12-month rolling mean as the solid line. Empty months: no sittings (left out, not 0). Hover for values.</p>
        <div class="card"><h3>Bundestag (a): share of speech sentences that are climate-relevant</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-share-a"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Bundestag (b): share of speeches with climate as main topic</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-share-b"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Public attention: Google Trends “Klimawandel” (search term, DE)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-trends-main"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Physical signal: global temperature anomaly (GISTEMP)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-global-main"></div></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Physical signal: climate-related disasters worldwide (EM-DAT)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-events-global"></div></div>
      </section>

      <section id="indexed">
        <h2>H1: smoothed and indexed</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          12-month rolling means as z-scores, so the four series share one axis. This compares shape, not size. All of them
          trend upward, which is why #12 removes trend and seasonality before any test.
        </p>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="legend">
            <span style="--c: var(--series-1)">Bundestag climate share</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-2)">Trends: Klimawandel</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-4)">Temperature global</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-3)">Disasters global</span>
          </div>
          <div class="chart" id="chart-indexed"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="trends">
        <h2>H1: Google Trends, all four exports</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          Each file was exported alone, so each has its own 0–100 scale: compare shapes, not levels. Dashed line: topic
          break {meta.trends_break}. Dots: the 3 months per series with the largest jump over the 6 months before and after.
        </p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {meta.trend_terms.map((t) => (
            <div class="card"><h3>{t}</h3><div class="chart" id={`chart-trend-${t}`}></div></div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>month</th><th>index</th><th>median of ±6 months</th><th>ratio</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.trends_spikes.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.term}</td><td>{d.month}</td><td>{d.index}</td><td>{num(d.median_around)}</td><td>{num(d.ratio, 2)}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="h2">
        <h2>H2: German vs global signal</h2>
        <p class="sub">Monthly deviation from the 1991–2020 mean of the same calendar month; warm months red, cool months blue, 12-month mean as line.</p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card"><h3>Temperature anomaly Germany (DWD)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-de"></div></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Temperature anomaly global (GISTEMP)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-temp-global"></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Disasters hitting Germany (EM-DAT)</h3>
          <p class="sub">{meta.months_zero_events_de} of {meta.months} months have no German event, so H2 may stay descriptive.</p>
          <div class="chart" id="chart-events-de"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Disasters in the rest of the world (EM-DAT)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-events-row"></div></div>
        {meta.has_heat && (
          <div class="card"><h3>Heat-related deaths in Germany (RKI, from 2016)</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-heat"></div></div>
        )}
      </section>

      <section id="h3">
        <h2>H3: parties and government vs opposition</h2>
        <p class="sub">Share of each party's speech sentences that are climate-relevant, per year (other parties in gray). Attention, not stance: stance follows once labels exist.</p>
        <div class="grid-4">
          {parties.map((p) => (
            <div class="card"><h3>{p}</h3><div class="chart" id={`chart-party-${p}`}></div></div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="legend">
            <span style="--c: var(--series-1)">government parties</span>
            <span style="--c: var(--series-2)">opposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="chart" id="chart-gov"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="units">
        <h2>Stance units per party: monthly or quarterly?</h2>
        <p class="sub">
          Climate-relevant sentences per party and period, only periods in which the party spoke. Stance windows are built
          around these sentences, so this is an upper bound for how many windows a party-month can have (H1b, H3).
        </p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {[["per month", unitsMonth], ["per quarter", unitsQuarter]].map(([title, rows]) => (
            <div class="card table-wrap">
              <h3>{title}</h3>
              <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>periods</th><th>with 0</th><th>p10</th><th>p25</th><th>median</th><th>total</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                  {(rows as typeof unitsMonth).map((d) => (
                    <tr><td>{d.party}</td><td>{d.periods}</td><td>{d.zero}</td><td>{num(d.p10)}</td><td>{num(d.p25)}</td><td>{num(d.median)}</td><td>{int(d.total)}</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="corpus">
        <h2>Corpus and join</h2>
        <p class="sub">All speech sentences per month (the denominator of attention a). Gaps are months without sittings; dashed lines mark new legislative periods.</p>
        <div class="card"><h3>Speech sentences per month</h3><div class="chart" id="chart-volume"></div></div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Legislative period</th><th>months</th><th>speeches</th><th>sentences</th><th>sentences per speech (mean / median)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.corpus_lp.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.lp}. Bundestag</td><td>{d.first_month} – {d.last_month}</td><td>{int(d.speeches)}</td><td>{int(d.sentences)}</td><td>{num(d.mean_sentences_per_speech)} / {num(d.median_sentences_per_speech)}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>speeches</th><th>sentences</th><th>share of sentences</th><th>relevant</th><th>climate share</th><th>main-topic speeches</th><th>sceptic markers</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.corpus_party.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.party}</td><td>{int(d.speeches)}</td><td>{int(d.sentences)}</td><td>{pct(d.share_of_sentences)}</td><td>{int(d.relevant)}</td><td>{pct(d.share_relevant)}</td><td>{int(d.main_topic_speeches)}</td><td>{int(d.sceptic)}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <h3>Join: months without a value per series</h3>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>months</th><th>missing</th><th>first</th><th>last</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {data.missing.map((d) => (
                <tr><td><code>{d.series}</code></td><td>{d.months}</td><td>{d.missing}</td><td>{d.first_month}</td><td>{d.last_month}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="top">
        <h2>Months with the highest climate share</h2>
        <p class="sub">Months with at least 2,000 speech sentences, so single short sittings don't dominate.</p>
        <div class="card table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>climate share (a)</th><th>main topic (b)</th><th>Trends: Klimawandel</th><th>anomaly global</th><th>disasters global</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {topMonths.map((d) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{d.month}</td><td>{pct(d.share_a)}</td><td>{pct(d.share_b)}</td><td>{d.trends_Klimawandel ?? "–"}</td>
                  <td>{d.anomaly_global?.toFixed(2) ?? "–"} °C</td><td>{d.events_global ?? "–"}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="notes">
        <h2>Notes and caveats</h2>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li>Every number on this page comes from <code>results/2_data_understanding/tables/07_*.csv</code>, written by <code>src/07_data_understanding.py</code>; the paper figures come from the same tables.</li>
          <li>Relevance comes from step 2 (<code>src/02_relevance.py</code>, #5); rerun this page when the keyword list changes.</li>
          <li>Rolling means are for display only. Months without sittings are left out of attention, not set to 0 (PR #19).</li>
          <li>Google Trends: each export has its own scale; the topic break and spike months are documented in <code>docs/context/data.md</code>.</li>
          <li>EM-DAT: the last months are incomplete (late reporting), and German heatwave deaths are not usable there; heat deaths come from the RKI (#26).</li>
          <li>Rebuild: <code>python src/07_data_understanding.py</code>, <code>python viz/export_data.py</code>, then <code>npm run dev</code> in <code>viz/</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>

    <script>
      import data from "../data/viz.json";
      import { render } from "../scripts/charts.js";

      const root = document.documentElement;
      try {
        const saved = localStorage.getItem("theme");
        if (saved) root.dataset.theme = saved;
      } catch {}
      document.getElementById("theme-toggle")!.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const dark = root.dataset.theme ? root.dataset.theme === "dark" : matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
        root.dataset.theme = dark ? "light" : "dark";
        try { localStorage.setItem("theme", root.dataset.theme); } catch {}
        render(data);
      });
      matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").addEventListener("change", () => render(data));

      render(data);

      let lastWidth = 0;
      let timer: number | undefined;
      new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
        const w = Math.round(entry.contentRect.width);
        if (w === lastWidth) return;
        lastWidth = w;
        clearTimeout(timer);
        timer = window.setTimeout(() => render(data), 120);
      }).observe(document.querySelector("main")!);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
